Q: How to get router context in createServerFn in Tanstack Start? A: <p>By looking at <a href="https://tanstack.com/start/latest/docs/framework/react/guide/authentication" rel="nofollow noreferrer">auth docs for Tanstack Start</a>, we can see that you can use hooks in server functions, take a look at one of their example</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'
import { redirect } from '@tanstack/react-router'

// Login server function
export const loginFn = createServerFn({ method: 'POST' })
  .inputValidator((data: { email: string; password: string }) =&gt; data)
  .handler(async ({ data }) =&gt; {
    // Verify credentials (replace with your auth logic)
    const user = await authenticateUser(data.email, data.password)

    if (!user) {
      return { error: 'Invalid credentials' }
    }

    // Create session
    const session = await useAppSession()
    await session.update({
      userId: user.id,
      email: user.email,
    })

    // Redirect to protected area
    throw redirect({ to: '/dashboard' })
  })
</code></pre>
<p>so, you can use the server function definition as below:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'

// Server function
export const loginFn = createServerFn({ method: 'POST' })
  .handler(async ({ data }) =&gt; {
    // use hooks
  })
</code></pre>
<p>Then, still in the same docs, in section <a href="https://tanstack.com/start/latest/docs/framework/react/guide/authentication#2-session-management" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Session Management</a>, you can see hook <code>useSession</code>, which might be exactly what you need. Example with this hook from the docs:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// utils/session.ts
import { useSession } from '@tanstack/react-start/server'

type SessionData = {
  userId?: string
  email?: string
  role?: string
}

export function useAppSession() {
  return useSession&lt;SessionData&gt;({
    // Session configuration
    name: 'app-session',
    password: process.env.SESSION_SECRET!, // At least 32 characters
    // Optional: customize cookie settings
    cookie: {
      secure: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production',
      sameSite: 'lax',
      httpOnly: true,
    },
  })
}
</code></pre>

Q: Support 16-KB Page size enablement on app encountered .so unsupported issue A: <p>The library that's causing your problem is the arm64-v8a copy of <code>libgmm-jni.so</code>. That's part of the Maps SDK for Android, and the copy you have is only 4KB aligned. A bit of googling reveals that someone already raised this in Google's <a href="https://issuetracker.google.com/issues/459586274" rel="nofollow noreferrer">issue tracker</a>.</p>
<p>The response from Google was:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>#1: Our testing shows that Maps SDK complies with the 16 KB page size.</p>
<p>Additionally, I don't expect that customers' apps contain <code>libgmm-jni.so</code>. Maps SDK's <em>client library</em> (<code>com.google.android.gms:play-services-maps</code>), which is included by apps during build, does not contain this file.</p>
<p><code>libgmm-jni.so</code> is a part of Maps SDK's <em>implementation</em>, which is contained in Google Play services, and called by apps during runtime.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The final response from the customer was:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I updated the packagingOptions block in my app's build.gradle to:</p>
</blockquote>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>        jniLibs {
            useLegacyPackaging = false
            // Keep native libraries uncompressed for proper 16 KB alignment
            // Note: LOAD section alignment is set during compilation, not packaging.
            // libgmm-jni.so comes from Google Play Services Maps (com.google.android.gms:play-services-maps:19.2.0)
            // and currently has 4 KB LOAD alignment. This is a Google library issue that will be
            // resolved by Google before the November 2025 deadline. Android automatically enables
            // 16 KB backcompat mode for apps with non-aligned libraries.
            keepDebugSymbols += ['**/*.so']
            excludes += ['**/libgmm-jni.so']
        }
        resources {
            excludes += ['build-data.properties', 'META-INF/LICENSE.md', 'META-INF/LICENSE-notice.md']
            excludes += ['**/libgmm-jni.so']
        }
    }
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>And the APK analyzer is now showing that the app is 16 KB aligned. Does this seem reasonable?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I suspect you should start by updating your Maps SDK, and if that doesn't fix it, try the fix that customer used.</p>
